+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

You may use OpenAI commercial services or misc/llama.cpp, for the last
one you should add into ~/.codex/config.toml:

	model_provider = "local"
	model = "local"
	web_search = "disabled"

	[model_providers.local]
	name = "llama-server"
	base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1"
	wire_api = "responses"

${PKGSTEM} uses a lot of memory, it starts with 256Mb per agent/worker
thread, and you may need to bump your limits (`ulimit -d`) or raise
datasize limit in used login class..

Also, ${PKGSTEM} may use a lot of open files, you may need to raise both
the system-wide maxfiles and used login class limits.

The local app-server daemon is enabled for eligible interactive sessions by
upstream's daemon_auto_start feature (enabled by default). It uses the same
executable as the CLI, installed at:

	${LOCALBASE}/libexec/codex/bin/codex

${LOCALBASE}/bin/codex is a link to that executable. The code-mode host is
installed beside it, and ${LOCALBASE}/libexec/codex/codex-package.json records
the package's version and native OpenBSD target. Codex checks the manifest and
executable versions before a new daemon start; no npm or Node.js is needed.

The daemon runs as the invoking user. Codex starts it on demand, detaches it
from the terminal, and leaves it running for other clients. Lifecycle commands:

	codex app-server daemon start
	codex app-server daemon version
	codex app-server daemon restart
	codex app-server daemon stop

PID records, logs, settings, and recovery data remain under CODEX_HOME
(default ~/.codex). The advertised socket is under app-server-control there;
on Unix it points to a protected socket under /tmp/codex-daemon-<uid>.
The daemon inherits the environment of the terminal that starts it, shared
by later clients. There is no system rc.d service and no restart after reboot.

The package manager owns the executable, host, and manifest. Use pkg_add -u
to update them, then `codex app-server daemon restart`. A running process keeps
its old image; an incompatible daemon version is reported with restart
instructions rather than being silently reused or internally replaced.
Restart also picks up port revisions with an unchanged upstream version.

Codex's daemon updater and --from-cli package replacement are disabled, not
the daemon. The /daemon update menu directs updates to pkg_add. No daemon
executable or package manifest is copied to ~/.codex/packages. Other optional
user-installed components and existing packages are left alone.

When migrating from an internally managed daemon, stop it with the old CLI
before replacing the package. No user-managed package files are deleted.
